  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
